---
import PublicContentLayout from '../../layouts/PublicContentLayout.astro';
import AgentTierMatrix from '../../components/AgentTierMatrix.astro';
import { integrationAgents } from '../../lib/agent-catalog';

export const prerender = true;

const title = 'Coding-Agent Integrations by Support Tier | Reinstate';
const description =
  'See Reinstate integrations at T1 and above: T5 encrypted sync for Claude Code and Codex CLI, plus T2 handoff sources. T0 catalog names stay on the matrix.';

const integrations = integrationAgents();
---

<PublicContentLayout
  {title}
  {description}
  path="/integrations"
  eyebrow="Coding-agent integrations"
  answer="Encrypted sync (T5) covers Claude Code and Codex CLI only, same-vendor. Gemini CLI, OpenCode, Grok Build, and Kimi Code CLI are T2 handoff sources. T0 catalog agents appear on the matrix with a reason; they do not have integration pages."
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Integrations', path: '/integrations' },
  ]}
  tags={['Claude Code', 'Codex CLI', 'coding-agent integrations', 'session sync']}
>
  <h2 id="available-integrations">Integrations at T1 and above</h2>
  <p>
    An integration page exists only when the catalog tier is T1 or higher. T0 names
    belong on the <a href="/compatibility">compatibility matrix</a>, not on a page that
    implies a working adapter.
  </p>

  <div class="pc-grid">
    {
      integrations.map((agent) => (
        <a class="pc-card" href={agent.integrationPath}>
          <h3>{agent.name}</h3>
          <p>
            {agent.tier} · {agent.resumeMode}
          </p>
          <span class="pc-link">Read {agent.name} integration →</span>
        </a>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h2 id="tier-matrix">Catalog tier matrix</h2>
  <p>
    Breadth is a matrix. Encrypted sync covers Claude Code and Codex CLI. The other
    catalog rows are T2 handoff sources or T0 known names.
  </p>
  <AgentTierMatrix caption="Catalog agent support tiers for integrations" />

  <h2 id="support-summary">What each published tier can do</h2>
  <div class="pc-table-wrap">
    <table class="pc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Capability</th>
          <th scope="col">Claude Code (T5)</th>
          <th scope="col">Codex CLI (T5)</th>
          <th scope="col">Gemini / OpenCode / Grok / Kimi (T2)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Local session discovery (T1)</td>
          <td>Included</td>
          <td>Included</td>
          <td>Included, read-only</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Handoff source (T2)</td>
          <td>Included</td>
          <td>Included</td>
          <td>Included</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Same-vendor native resume (T3)</td>
          <td>Included</td>
          <td>Included</td>
          <td>Not supported</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Handoff destination (T4)</td>
          <td>Included</td>
          <td>Included</td>
          <td>Not supported</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Encrypted push and pull (T5)</td>
          <td>Same-vendor sessions</td>
          <td>Same-vendor sessions</td>
          <td>Not supported</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Native cross-agent translation</td>
          <td>Not supported</td>
          <td>Not supported</td>
          <td>Not supported</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>MCP, skills, plugins, or settings sync</td>
          <td>Later roadmap</td>
          <td>Later roadmap</td>
          <td>Later roadmap</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Reinstate fails closed for recognized but untested agent versions: discovery can remain
    read-only, while export and restore are blocked. See the exact ranges and release evidence on
    the <a href="/compatibility">compatibility page</a> and see when those ranges changed in the
    <a href="/compatibility/agent-version-history">agent-version history</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="integration-flow">How a T5 integration moves a session</h2>
  <ol class="pc-steps">
    <li>
      <strong>Discover.</strong> An adapter finds session artifacts in the agent's local,
      vendor-owned data layout.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Normalize.</strong> Reinstate replaces known structural absolute paths with portable
      project tokens. It does not rewrite free-form transcript prose.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Encrypt and store.</strong> The portable artifact is sealed locally with age
      passphrase encryption before it is uploaded to your S3-compatible bucket.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Restore.</strong> On the destination device, Reinstate expands project tokens using
      that device's configured roots and writes to the native agent layout with backups and
      conflict handling.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Resume natively.</strong> You continue using the same agent vendor. Reinstate remains
      a continuity layer, not the place where the agent executes.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <div class="pc-callout">
    <p>Native resume is same-vendor only.</p>
    <p>
      Reinstate does not silently translate a Claude Code transcript into a Codex rollout or vice
      versa. Explicit structured handoff starts a <em>new</em> Claude Code or Codex session with a
      visible, lossy checkpoint.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="not-yet-supported">T0 names are not integrations</h2>
  <p>
    Pi, Qwen Code, Cursor CLI, Cline, Roo Code, Aider, GitHub Copilot CLI,
    Amp, OpenHands, and ZCode are T0. They appear in <code>rein doctor --agents</code> with a
    machine-readable reason such as <code>layout_unverified</code>, <code>server_backed</code>,
    or <code>desktop_only</code>. That is a complete result, not a preview of sync.
  </p>
  <p>
    Reinstate is independent of Anthropic, OpenAI, Google, xAI, and the other vendors named
    here. Reading local vendor session files does not imply a partnership, endorsement, or
    access to vendor accounts.
  </p>

  <section class="pc-related" aria-labelledby="related-heading">
    <h2 id="related-heading">Continue exploring</h2>
    <nav aria-label="Related pages">
      <a href="/compatibility">Compatibility matrix</a>
      <a href="/security">Security model</a>
      <a href="/docs/adapters">Adapter documentation</a>
      <a href="/docs/getting-started">Getting started</a>
    </nav>
  </section>
</PublicContentLayout>
